{"book":"PSA","chapter":125,"source":"hbo","verses":[1,2,3,4,5],"records":[{"id":"PSA.125.1.s1","verses":[1],"source":"שִׁ֗יר הַֽמַּ֫עֲל֥וֹתהַבֹּטְחִ֥ים בַּיהוָ֑ה כְּֽהַר־צִיּ֥וֹן לֹא־יִ֝מּ֗וֹט לְעוֹלָ֥ם יֵשֵֽׁב׃","gloss":"The song of the ascents. Those who trust in YHWH [are] like Mount Zion, not it will be shaken, forever it will remain.","pieces":[[1,"A song of ascents. Whoever trusts in {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} is like Mount Zion: nothing can shake it; it stays in place forever.",null]],"L":{"1":"A song of ascents. Those who trust in {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} [are] like Mount Zion, [which] will not be shaken; it remains forever."},"R":{"1":"A song for the journey up to Jerusalem. People who trust {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} are like Mount Zion. Nothing can shake that mountain. It will stand forever."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"punctuation","tokens":["o191250010011","o191250010021","o191250010022"],"choice":"The title shares verse 1","alternatives":[],"reason":"As in 120:1, the short title and the first line are counted together as verse 1, so the verse numbers are the same as in Bibles that print the title without a number.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o191250010011","o191250010022"],"choice":"a song of ascents: probably a pilgrim song","alternatives":[],"reason":"As in 120:1: one of the fifteen songs probably sung by pilgrims going up to Jerusalem.","uncertain":true,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o191250010052","o191250010061"],"choice":"Mount Zion: the hill of the temple","alternatives":[],"reason":"Zion is the hill in Jerusalem where the temple stood, the place God chose to live among his people (2:6; 132:13-14).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m4","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191250010101"],"choice":"remain: stay in place","alternatives":["be inhabited","sit enthroned"],"reason":"The verb (yashav) means to sit, stay or dwell. Here it means the mountain stays where it is; some take it as \"is inhabited forever\".","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"word_choice","tokens":["o191250010011","o191250010022"],"choice":"A song for the journey up to Jerusalem","alternatives":["A song of ascents"],"reason":"As in 120:1.","uncertain":true,"footnote":false}],"heading":"Like Mount Zion, which cannot be shaken","status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.125.2.s1","verses":[2],"source":"יְֽרוּשָׁלִַ֗ם הָרִים֮ סָבִ֪יב לָ֥הּ וַ֭יהוָה סָבִ֣יב לְעַמּ֑וֹ מֵ֝עַתָּ֗ה וְעַד־עוֹלָֽם׃","gloss":"Jerusalem — mountains [are] around it, and YHWH [is] around his people from now and until forever.","pieces":[[2,"Mountains ring Jerusalem all around, and {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} is all around {{deity_pronoun:his|His}} people, from now on and forever.",null]],"L":{"2":"Jerusalem — mountains [are] around her, and {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} [is] around {{deity_pronoun:his|His}} people from now and until forever."},"R":{"2":"The hills stand all around Jerusalem. In the same way, {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} is all around {{deity_pronoun:his|His}} people to protect them, now and forever."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o191250020011","o191250020021"],"choice":"the mountains around Jerusalem","alternatives":[],"reason":"Jerusalem stands on hills with higher hills around it, such as the Mount of Olives to the east. The hills were a natural defense, and the LORD is a better one.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["B","R"],"category":"word_choice","tokens":["o191250020042"],"choice":"Jerusalem","alternatives":["her"],"reason":"In the Hebrew a city is feminine, \"her\"; Balanced and Readable name the city, Literal keeps \"her\".","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-2","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"supplied_words","tokens":["o191250020061"],"choice":"to protect them","alternatives":[],"reason":"Readable says what the picture of surrounding means.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.125.3.s1","verses":[3],"source":"כִּ֤י לֹ֪א יָנ֡וּחַ שֵׁ֤בֶט הָרֶ֗שַׁע עַל֮ גּוֹרַ֪ל הַֽצַּדִּ֫יקִ֥ים לְמַ֡עַן לֹא־יִשְׁלְח֖וּ הַצַּדִּיקִ֨ים בְּעַוְלָ֬תָה יְדֵיהֶֽם׃","gloss":"For not will rest [the] scepter of wickedness on [the] lot of the righteous, so that not will stretch out the righteous in injustice their hands.","pieces":[[3,"Wicked rule will not stay in place over the land that belongs to the righteous, or even the righteous might reach out their hands to do wrong.",null]],"L":{"3":"For the scepter of wickedness will not rest on the lot of the righteous, so that the righteous will not stretch out their hands to injustice."},"R":{"3":"Wicked rulers will not always control the land of people who do what is right. If they did, even good people might start doing wrong."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191250030041","o191250030052"],"choice":"the scepter of wickedness: wicked rule","alternatives":["the rod of the wicked"],"reason":"A scepter (shevet) is the rod of a ruler. The picture is of wicked people, perhaps foreign rulers, governing God's people.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o191250030071"],"choice":"the lot: the land given by lot","alternatives":[],"reason":"Israel's land was divided among the tribes and families by lot (Joshua 14:2; 18:10), so \"lot\" means the land a family received as its share.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o191250030091","o191250030101"],"choice":"so that ... not: to keep the righteous from wrong","alternatives":[],"reason":"The LORD will not let wicked rule last, so that the righteous are not pushed into doing wrong themselves under its pressure.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["B","R"],"category":"word_choice","tokens":["o191250030082","o191250030122"],"choice":"the righteous (B); people who do what is right, good people (R)","alternatives":[],"reason":"As in Psalms 1-12, Readable says \"people who do what is right\" for the righteous.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.125.4.s1","verses":[4],"source":"הֵיטִ֣יבָה יְ֭הוָה לַטּוֹבִ֑ים וְ֝לִֽישָׁרִ֗ים בְּלִבּוֹתָֽם׃","gloss":"Do good, YHWH, to the good, and to [the] upright in their hearts.","pieces":[[4,"{{divine_name:LORD|Yahweh}}, be good to good people, to all whose hearts are upright.",null]],"L":{"4":"Do good, {{divine_name:LORD|Yahweh}}, to the good, and to [those who are] upright in their hearts."},"R":{"4":"{{divine_name:LORD|Yahweh}}, be good to people who are good and who want to do what is right."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191250040043","o191250040052"],"choice":"upright in their hearts: honest within","alternatives":[],"reason":"The upright (yesharim) are straight, not crooked; \"in their hearts\" means in their thoughts and intentions. Verse 5 sets them against those who turn to crooked ways.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"word_choice","tokens":["o191250040043","o191250040052"],"choice":"who want to do what is right","alternatives":["upright in heart"],"reason":"Readable says what an upright heart is (m1).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.125.5.s1","verses":[5],"source":"וְהַמַּטִּ֤ים עַֽקַלְקַלּוֹתָ֗ם יוֹלִיכֵ֣ם יְ֭הוָה אֶת־פֹּעֲלֵ֣י הָאָ֑וֶן שָׁ֝ל֗וֹם עַל־יִשְׂרָאֵֽל׃","gloss":"And [those who] turn aside [to] their crooked [ways], YHWH will lead them away with [the] doers of wickedness. Peace [be] on Israel.","pieces":[[5,"But as for people who wander off onto crooked paths, {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} will drive them away with all who do evil. May Israel have peace!",null]],"L":{"5":"But [as for] those who turn aside [to] their crooked [ways], {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} will lead them away with the doers of wickedness. Peace [be] upon Israel."},"R":{"5":"But some people leave the right road and follow crooked paths. {{divine_name:The LORD|Yahweh}} will send them away with everyone who does evil. May Israel have peace!"},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191250050021"],"choice":"crooked ways: twisted paths","alternatives":[],"reason":"The word ('aqalqallot) means winding or twisted; it is used of crooked paths (Judges 5:6). It is the opposite of the upright of verse 4.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191250050031"],"choice":"lead them away: drive out, as captives","alternatives":["banish"],"reason":"The verb (halak, causative) means to lead away; the same word is used of being led off into exile or to judgment.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o191250050081","o191250050101"],"choice":"peace on Israel: a closing blessing","alternatives":[],"reason":"The psalm ends with a blessing on the whole people, as 128:6 does.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"word_choice","tokens":["o191250050013"],"choice":"leave the right road and follow crooked paths","alternatives":["turn aside to their crooked ways"],"reason":"Readable spells out the picture of turning off a straight road (m1).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"}],"words":{"1":[{"id":"o191250010011",